================================================================================
NAME
       operators - resonance-aware computational units executed inside triads
                   within the RTT-Inside Engine

SYNOPSIS
       Operators define:
           • computational behavior
           • resonance signatures
           • structural transitions
           • schema-aligned inputs and outputs
           • triad-safe execution rules

DESCRIPTION
       Operators are the executable units of RTT. They perform the actual
       computation inside the RTT-Inside Engine, but only after being wrapped
       in a triadic cycle and validated by WRSADC.

       Operators are not arbitrary functions. They are resonance-aware,
       schema-defined transformations that must obey triadic structure and
       produce lineage-consistent results.

       Every operator has:
           • a resonance signature
           • an operator-schema
           • a structural role inside a triad
           • lineage obligations
           • coherence constraints

OPERATOR STRUCTURE
       An operator consists of:

           1. signature
                  Defines input types, output types, and resonance behavior.

           2. schema
                  Declares structural boundaries and allowed transitions.

           3. body
                  The computational logic executed by the Engine.

           4. metadata
                  Resonance hints, lineage tags, and compatibility markers.

       Example (conceptual):

              operator add_velocity:
                  signature:
                      input:  state_vector
                      output: state_vector
                      resonance: linear-shift
                  schema:
                      allowed_patterns: [core, sequential]
                  body:
                      v_new = v_old + delta

RESONANCE SIGNATURES
       A resonance signature describes how an operator interacts with
       resonance-time. It defines:

           • how resonance shifts during execution
           • how the operator affects coherence
           • how results propagate across cycles
           • how the operator composes with other operators

       Common resonance signatures include:

           linear-shift
                  Applies a uniform resonance adjustment.

           harmonic
                  Produces oscillatory or periodic resonance effects.

           integrative
                  Accumulates resonance across cycles.

           dissipative
                  Reduces resonance amplitude or complexity.

           refractive
                  Alters resonance direction or interpretation.

       WRSADC uses resonance signatures to validate operator compatibility.

OPERATORS INSIDE TRIADS
       Operators never run alone. They always execute inside a triad:

           P1 (begin)
               → operator receives initial resonance state
           P2 (transform)
               → operator performs its main computation
               → resonance signature is applied
           P3 (close)
               → operator results are integrated into lineage

       This ensures:
           • structural safety
           • resonance coherence
           • schema alignment
           • reproducible lineage

       Operators that bypass triads are considered unsafe.

INTERACTION WITH SCHEMAS
       Operators must satisfy:

           • operator-schema
           • structural-schema
           • resonance-schema
           • lineage-schema
           • package-schema

       Schema violations cause WRSADC to reject dispatch.

INTERACTION WITH LINEAGE
       Operators produce lineage entries that include:

           • operator name
           • resonance signature applied
           • structural transitions
           • triad phase
           • input/output summaries
           • schema validation results

       Lineage ensures that operator effects remain coherent across cycles.

INTERACTION WITH WRSADC
       WRSADC is responsible for:

           • validating operator schemas
           • injecting structural-awareness metadata
           • wrapping operators in triads
           • ensuring resonance-safe dispatch
           • updating lineage

       Operators never interact with the Engine directly.

INTERACTION WITH THE ENGINE
       Once validated and triad-wrapped, operators:

           • execute inside the Engine
           • apply resonance signatures
           • update internal RT-state
           • propagate structural effects

       The Engine assumes operators are schema-aligned and resonance-safe.

OPERATOR LIFECYCLE
       1. Author defines operator-schema.
       2. Operator logic is implemented.
       3. Operator is added to a resonance package.
       4. WRSADC validates the operator.
       5. Operator is wrapped in a triad.
       6. Operator executes inside the Engine.
       7. Lineage is updated.

NOTES
       Operators are the computational heart of RTT. They transform resonance,
       propagate structure, and evolve state — but only when protected by
       schemas, lineage, and triadic structure.

